>
  <p>Studying at home sounds easy and is often the hardest place of all. There is no bell to start you, no teacher watching, and a fridge, a bed and a phone all within reach. Effective home study is really about rebuilding the structure that school hands you for free.</p>

  <h2 id="space">Set up a study space</h2>
  <p>Pick one spot you use only for study: a desk, a kitchen table, a corner. Keep it tidy and stocked with what you need so starting takes no setup. Avoid your bed, where your brain expects to switch off. A consistent space becomes a cue that tells your mind it is time to work, which is self-regulation made physical.</p>

  <h2 id="structure">Give the day structure</h2>
  <p>Decide the night before exactly when you will start and what the first task is. Gollwitzer's if-then plans ("after breakfast, at my desk, I start maths") roughly double follow-through. Lay the day out in the <a href="/tools/timetable-maker/">timetable maker</a>, break it into <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">timed blocks</a>, and set a daily target with the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> so you know when you are done.</p>

  <h2 id="people">Handle people at home</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Announce your blocks.</strong> "I am studying until 4, free after" turns constant interruptions into planned ones.</li>
    <li><strong>Use a visible signal</strong> such as a closed door or headphones.</li>
    <li><strong>Batch family time</strong> into your breaks so you are not torn between the two.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Gloria Mark's interruption research shows how expensive each break in concentration is, so preventing them is worth the awkward conversation.</p>

  <h2 id="traps">Avoid the home traps</h2>
  <p>The classic home traps are the phone, the bed, the "quick" snack that becomes an hour, and open-ended days that never properly start or end. Put the phone in another room, keep a finish time, and if you catch yourself "studying" all day while absorbing nothing, stop and reset with a short, defined block. For the distraction side specifically, see <a href="/guides/how-to-avoid-distractions-while-studying/">how to avoid distractions while studying</a>.</p>
</Guide>
